// DYNAMIC.md §4.7 / §7: assigning to a list element `e[i] = v` writes through
// the index in place. The target is an l-value only — no read is materialised,
// so the offset expression is emitted once, in store position
// (`xs[(0) + 1] = 10`), unlike the compound form which emits it on both sides
// (see 4-07-list-compound-assign). Lua adjusts the offset by one. A variable
// index exercises the runtime-resolved write. The list starts `[1, 2, 3]` and
// ends `[10, 20, 30]`, so the golden proves the writes landed, not that
// construction alone produced the values.
extern {
    fn println(n: number);
}

fn main() {
    let xs = [1, 2, 3];
    xs[0] = 10;
    xs[1] = 20;
    let i = 2;
    xs[i] = 30;
    println(xs[0]);
    println(xs[1]);
    println(xs[2]);
}
